# Angular security

Stack-specific risks on top of the common security rules, focused on where
Angular's own sanitization can be bypassed and where DI/routing choices
create an opening.

## Template injection and sanitization bypass

- Angular auto-sanitizes values bound into `innerHTML`, `href`,
  style/URL/resource-URL contexts, and script/style properties — do not
  reach for `DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml`/`bypassSecurityTrustUrl`/
  `bypassSecurityTrustResourceUrl`/`bypassSecurityTrustScript` on
  user-supplied or user-influenced content. Those calls tell Angular "trust
  this value completely, skip sanitization" — reserve them for genuinely
  static, developer-authored content (a hardcoded SVG, a known-safe
  asset URL), never for a value that traces back to user input or an
  external API response.
- Prefer binding through the normal property/interpolation syntax
  (`{{ value }}`, `[textContent]`) over `[innerHTML]` for user-controlled
  text; if `[innerHTML]` is genuinely required for rich content, sanitize
  server-side or via a vetted sanitizer library rather than trusting
  Angular's default sanitizer alone for anything beyond simple formatting.
- Never write directly to `ElementRef.nativeElement.innerHTML` — that
  bypasses Angular's sanitizer entirely, unlike the `[innerHTML]` template
  binding which still runs through it.

## HTTP and CSRF

- Send credentials/tokens via `HttpClient` interceptors (`HttpInterceptorFn`
  or a class-based `HttpInterceptor`), not by hand-appending an
  `Authorization` header at each call site — a missed call site is a
  silent auth gap, not a build error.
- When the backend uses cookie-based sessions, rely on Angular's built-in
  `XSRF-TOKEN`/`X-XSRF-TOKEN` CSRF handling (`withXsrfConfiguration` /
  the default cookie name) rather than disabling `withCredentials` or
  rolling a custom CSRF header scheme, unless the backend's own CSRF
  contract genuinely requires different header/cookie names — configure
  those, don't drop the protection.

## Routing and route guards

- Gate any route that requires authentication/authorization with a route
  guard (`CanActivateFn`/`CanMatchFn`) that actually checks server-verified
  state (a token's validity, a role from the authenticated session) — never
  a guard that only checks a client-side flag with no corresponding
  server-side check on the API the route's data comes from.
- Do not put secrets, API keys, or backend-only credentials into
  environment files (`environment.ts`) that ship in the client bundle —
  anything in `environment.*.ts` is public once built; only put
  publicly-safe configuration (API base URLs, feature flags) there.

## Dependency injection surface

- Do not widen a service's provider scope to `providedIn: 'root'` just to
  work around an injection error, when the service holds per-user or
  per-session state — a root-scoped singleton is shared across the whole
  app's lifetime (and across users in SSR), so session-scoped data leaking
  into it is a cross-request data leak, not just a design smell.
- Validate and narrow data crossing a trust boundary (route params, query
  params, a form's raw value, a third-party postMessage payload) before
  using it in a template binding or passing it to a service call — treat
  router/query-string values as untrusted input, not as pre-validated
  application state.
